{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:'Recap. First, the cutoff gain is zero point seven oh seven. Second, a current locus is a circle, V over the fixed element across. Third, M equals k root L one L two.',cues:{r1:'First, the cutoff',r2:'Second, a current',r3:'Third, M equals'},panels:[["Frequency response","BLU",'ctRecF',['Cutoff: |H| = 0.707, −3 dB.','Band-pass: BW = f₀/Q.'],'r1'],["Locus diagrams","ORG",'ctRecL',['Current tip: a circle.','Diameter = V / fixed element.'],'r2'],["Coupled coils","GRN",'ctRecK',['M = k√(L₁L₂)','0 ≤ k ≤ 1'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice: the book's own questions and model answers ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is the half-power point also called the −3 dB point?",say:'Question one. Why is the half-power point also called the minus three decibel point?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why is the'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why is the half-power point also called the −3 dB point?",say:'At half power, gain squared is one half. Ten log of one half is minus three point oh one decibels. So the amplitude is zero point seven oh seven.',cues:{a1:'At half power',a2:'Ten log of',a3:'So the amplitude'},al:[["At half power, |H|² = 1/2.",'a1'],["In dB, 10 log₁₀(1/2) = −3.01 dB.",'a2'],["The amplitude is 1/√2 = 0.707.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${CT_L(40,440,580,440,CT_AX,2)}${CT_T(310,40,'power and amplitude at the cutoff',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle'})}
${g('rise',tk(c,'a1'),CT_box(80,440-340,130,340,{fill:'#E7F5FF',st:BLU,r:6})+CT_box(240,440-170,130,170,{fill:'#D3F9D8',st:GRN,r:6}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_T(145,88,'1',{fs:28,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU})+CT_T(305,258,'|H|² = ½',{fs:28,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN})+CT_T(145,476,'pass band',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT})+CT_T(305,476,'power',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_T(305,300,'−3.01 dB',{fs:28,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:GRN}))}
${g('rise',tk(c,'a3'),CT_box(400,440-340*Math.SQRT1_2,130,340*Math.SQRT1_2,{fill:'#FFF3BF',st:ORG,r:6}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a3'),CT_T(465,440-340*Math.SQRT1_2-12,'|H| = 0.707',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:ORG})+CT_T(465,476,'amplitude',{fs:22,fw:700,a:'middle',fill:CT_MUT}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"An RC low-pass has R = 2.2 kΩ and C = 10 nF. Find fc.",say:'Question two. Two point two kilohms and ten nano farads, in an RC low-pass. Find the cutoff.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'Two point two'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"An RC low-pass has R = 2.2 kΩ and C = 10 nF. Find fc.",say:'First, R C is two point two times ten to the minus five seconds. Then one over two pi R C gives seven point two three kilohertz.',cues:{fk:'First, R C',k1:'First, R C',k1b:'two point two',k2:'Then one over',k2b:'seven point two'},calc:{f:['fc = 1/(2πRC)'],hint:'2.2 kΩ = 2200 Ω;  10 nF = 10 × 10⁻⁹ F.',fk:'fk',steps:[["RC",["RC = 2200 × 10 × 10⁻⁹","RC = 2.2 × 10⁻⁵ s"],["k1","k1b"]],["Cutoff",["fc = 1/(2π × 2.2 × 10⁻⁵) = 1/(1.382 × 10⁻⁴)","fc = 7234 Hz ≈ 7.23 kHz"],["k2","k2b"]]],live:[0,1]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"At f = fc, what are the gain in dB and the phase of an RC low-pass?",say:'Question three. At the cutoff of an RC low-pass, what are the gain in decibels and the phase?',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'At the cutoff'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"At f = fc, what are the gain in dB and the phase of an RC low-pass?",say:'The gain is twenty log of zero point seven oh seven: minus three point oh one decibels. The phase is minus forty-five degrees: the output lags.',cues:{a1:'The gain is',a2:'The phase is'},al:[["Gain = 20 log₁₀(0.707) = −3.01 dB.",'a1'],["Phase = −45°: the output lags the input by 45°.",'a2']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${CT_L(40,180,600,180,CT_AX,2)}${CT_L(80,60,80,460,CT_AX,2)}${CT_T(310,40,'phasors at f = fc',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'middle'})}
${CT_arrow(80,180,520,180,ORG,6)}${CT_T(520,164,CT_sub('V','in'),{fs:28,fw:800,a:'end',fill:ORG})}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_arrow(80,180,80+440*0.5,180+440*0.5,BLU,6)+CT_T(80+440*0.5+14,180+440*0.5+8,CT_sub('V','out')+' = 0.707 '+CT_sub('V','in'),{fs:26,fw:800,fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),`<path d="M${80+150} 180 A150 150 0 0 1 ${(80+150*Math.SQRT1_2).toFixed(1)} ${(180+150*Math.SQRT1_2).toFixed(1)}" fill="none" stroke="${GRN}" stroke-width="4"/>`+CT_T(250,250,'45° lag',{fs:28,fw:800,fill:GRN}))}`},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are linked in the description. Redraw the filter curves, the locus circles and the two coils.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the filter'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["The three chapters", "Frequency response of RC and RLC circuits; locus diagrams of RL, RC and RLC circuits; self and mutual inductance and the coefficient of coupling"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["RC and RLC frequency response", "The RC low-pass and high-pass are 3 dB down at fc = 1591.5 Hz; the band-pass peaks at 1 kHz, is 100 Hz wide and passes 0.263 at 1.2 kHz."], ["Locus diagrams", "The RL current runs round a semicircle of diameter 10 A, with 500 W at R = 10 Ω; the coil circle lifted by 10 A cuts V at 3.33 A and 30 A."], ["Two coupled coils", "L₁ = 4 H and L₂ = 1 H with k = 0.5, so M = 1 H: a 2 A/s change gives 8 V in coil 1 and 2 V in coil 2."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: William H. Hayt, Jack E. Kemmerly and Steven M. Durbin (Engineering Circuit Analysis); Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku (Fundamentals of Electric Circuits); M. E. Van Valkenburg (Network Analysis)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: minus three decibels is zero point seven oh seven, a current locus is a circle, and M is k root L one L two. Next time, the dot convention and coupled coils. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'minus three decibels',w2:'a current locus',w3:'and M is',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['−3 dB: |H| = 0.707','BLU','w1'],['LOCUS: A CIRCLE','ORG','w2'],['M = k√(L₁L₂)','GRN','w3']],next:["NEXT TIME", "Next lesson", "Dot convention and analysis of coupled coils", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
